      "text": "can be hectic, with numerous life-threatening incidents to attend to; they'll\nenter burning homes to rescue trapped residents, and assist with medical\nemergencies. These ups and downs presented the perfect setting for an\nexperiment on how people's ability to use information changes when they feel\nunder pressure.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        228.1547088623047,
        520.172119140625,
        286.6736145019531
      ],
      "text": "We found that perceived threat acted as a trigger for a stress reaction that\nmade the task of processing information easier for the firefighters - but only as\nlong as it conveyed bad news.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        331.59368896484375,
        521.1817626953125,
        449.75360107421875
      ],
      "text": "This is how we arrived at these results. We asked the firefighters to estimate\ntheir likelihood of experiencing 40 different adverse events in their life, such as\nbeing involved in an accident or becoming a victim of card fraud. We then gave\nthem either good news (that their likelihood of experiencing these events was\nlower than they'd thought) or bad news (that it was higher) and asked them to\nprovide new estimates.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        494.6746826171875,
        519.65234375,
        652.5526123046875
      ],
      "text": "People are normally quite optimistic - they will ignore bad news and embrace\nthe good. This is what happened when the firefighters were relaxed; but when\nthey were under stress, a different pattern emerged. Under these conditions,\nthey became hyper-vigilant to bad news, even when it had nothing to do with\ntheir job (such as learning that the likelihood of card fraud was higher than\nthey'd thought), and altered their beliefs in response. In contrast, stress didn't\nchange how they responded to good news (such as learning that the likelihood\nof card fraud was lower than they'd thought).\n"
    },
